.TH "DIAG_FC" "8" "2026" "Linux" "PowerLinux Diagnostic Tools"
.hy
.SH NAME
.PP
diag_fc \- collect FC adapter failure evidence on PowerVM LPARs
.SH SYNOPSIS
.PP
\f[B]diag_fc\f[] \f[B]\-p\f[] \f[I]pci_addr\f[]
[\f[B]\-t\f[] \f[I]type\f[]]
[\f[B]\-s\f[] \f[I]source\f[]]
[\f[B]\-r\f[] \f[I]reason\f[]]
[\f[B]\-T\f[] \f[I]timestamp\f[]]
[\f[B]\-o\f[] \f[I]outfile\f[]]
.PD 0
.P
.PD
\f[B]diag_fc\f[] \f[B]\-\-help\f[]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
\f[B]diag_fc\f[] is a passive evidence collector for Fibre Channel Host
Bus Adapter (HBA) permanent failures on PowerVM LPAR systems.
It is invoked by an external component \(em such as the EEH subsystem or
platform firmware \(em after a permanent FC adapter failure has been
confirmed.
It collects adapter identity, port state, driver information, VPD data,
IBM location code, and machine context into a structured JSON report.
.PP
\f[B]diag_fc\f[] does \f[B]not\f[] detect failures, monitor FC health,
or run continuously.
All sysfs and device\-tree access is strictly read\-only.
If the adapter has already disappeared from sysfs by the time
\f[B]diag_fc\f[] is invoked, it collects whatever partial data remains
and writes a partial report rather than failing.
.PP
The JSON report is written to a file or to stdout and is intended for
consumption by support tooling and call\-home infrastructure.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-p \f[I]pci_addr\f[]
.PD 0
.P
.PD
PCI address of the affected adapter function in
\f[I]DDDD:BB:DD.F\f[] format (e.g.\& \f[I]0155:90:00.0\f[]).
This option is required.
\f[B]diag_fc\f[] automatically discovers all sibling PCI functions on
the same physical slot and collects per\-port data for each.
.TP
.B \-t \f[I]type\f[]
.PD 0
.P
.PD
Trigger type string recorded in the report.
Default: \f[B]EEH_PERMANENT_FAILURE\f[].
.TP
.B \-s \f[I]source\f[]
.PD 0
.P
.PD
Trigger source string (e.g.\& \f[I]platform\f[], \f[I]manual\f[]).
Default: \f[B]manual\f[].
.TP
.B \-r \f[I]reason\f[]
.PD 0
.P
.PD
Human\-readable failure reason recorded in the report.
.TP
.B \-T \f[I]timestamp\f[]
.PD 0
.P
.PD
Trigger event time in ISO\ 8601 UTC format
(e.g.\& \f[I]2026\-06\-24T18:30:00Z\f[]).
If omitted, the collection time is used.
.TP
.B \-o \f[I]outfile\f[]
.PD 0
.P
.PD
Path of the JSON output file.
Use \f[B]\-\f[] or omit this option to write to stdout.
Default: stdout.
.TP
.B \-h, \-\-help
.PD 0
.P
.PD
Print usage information and exit.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Report written successfully.
.TP
.B 1
Invalid command\-line usage or unsupported platform.
.TP
.B 2
Fatal collection error (pci_addr or output struct is NULL).
.TP
.B 3
Report\-writing error (output file could not be opened or written).
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
Collect evidence after an EEH permanent failure detected by the platform:
.PP
.nf
\f[B]diag_fc \-p 0155:90:00.0 \e
        \-t EEH_PERMANENT_FAILURE \e
        \-s platform \e
        \-r "I/O adapter permanently unavailable" \e
        \-o /var/log/ppc64\-diag/fc_incident.json\f[]
.fi
.PP
Quick manual smoke test against a healthy adapter (stdout):
.PP
.nf
\f[B]diag_fc \-p 0155:90:00.0 \-t TEST \-s manual\f[]
.fi
.SH OUTPUT FORMAT
.PP
The report is a JSON object with the following top\-level sections:
.TP
.B schema_version
Format version string (\f[I]"1.0"\f[]).
.TP
.B trigger
Trigger type, source, PCI function, reason, reference, and timestamp
supplied by the caller.
.TP
.B machine
Hostname, OS name, kernel release, kernel build string, and architecture.
.TP
.B adapter
Symbolic name, PCI vendor/device/subsystem IDs, driver name and version,
IBM location code, VPD collection status, and VPD fields (part number,
FRU part number, serial number, CCIN, feature code, firmware level,
EC level).
.TP
.B ports
Array of per\-port records.
Each entry contains the PCI function address, fc_host name, presence
flags (\f[I]pci_function_present\f[], \f[I]fc_host_present\f[]),
WWPN, WWNN, port state, port type, port ID, speed, supported speeds,
and fabric name.
.TP
.B collection
UTC timestamp of when evidence collection completed.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B \f[I]/etc/ppc64\-diag/diag_fc.config\f[]
Optional configuration file.
\f[B]diag_fc\f[] operates correctly if this file is absent.
No threshold or monitoring settings are currently used; the file is
reserved for future use (e.g.\& output directory, report size limit).
.RS
.RE
.TP
.B \f[I]/sys/class/fc_host/\f[]
Sysfs directory from which per\-port FC attributes are read
(WWPN, WWNN, port state, speed, fabric name, symbolic name).
.RS
.RE
.TP
.B \f[I]/sys/bus/pci/devices/\f[]
Sysfs directory from which PCI identity attributes and binary VPD
are read.
.RS
.RE
.TP
.B \f[I]/sys/firmware/devicetree/base/\f[]
Device tree used to resolve the IBM location code for the adapter slot.
.RS
.RE
.TP
.B \f[I]/etc/cron.daily/run_diag_fc\f[]
Installed cron script.
This script is a no\-op placeholder.
\f[B]diag_fc\f[] is a passive collector and has no meaningful periodic
action; it must be invoked with \f[B]\-p\f[] by an external trigger.
.RS
.RE
.SH NOTES
.PP
\f[B]diag_fc\f[] only runs on PowerVM LPAR systems
(\f[I]PLATFORM_PSERIES_LPAR\f[]).
It exits with status\ 1 on any other platform.
.PP
VPD keyword\-to\-field mappings (MN\[->]serial_number, CC\[->]ccin,
PN\[->]part_number, FN\[->]fru_part_number, RM\[->]firmware_level,
EC\[->]ec_level) are candidates pending verification against the
binary VPD of each target adapter model.
If the \f[I]vpd_status\f[] field in the report is \f[I]"parse_error"\f[]
or \f[I]"unavailable"\f[], run
.B lspci\ \-vv\ \f[I]pci_addr\f[]
to inspect the raw VPD keywords and compare against the mappings in
\f[B]collect_vpd()\f[] in \f[I]diag_fc.c\f[].
.SH REPORTING BUGS
.PP
